<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hugh d'Avranches, Earl of Chester</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1047–1101</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Hugh Lupus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hugh d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a> was a Norman nobleman, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>, who became Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> in 1071. Ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a> with near-regal powers, he and his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-of-rhuddlan">Robert of Rhuddlan</a> pushed into north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and for a time dominated Gwynedd. Known as Hugh Lupus ('the wolf'), he refounded St Werburgh's at Chester as an abbey in 1093.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugh-davranches-earl-of-chester.jpg" alt="Hugh d'Avranches, Earl of Chester" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Man vyi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avranches_Octobre_2010_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
